Subject: Dropping a function's compiled binding moves the compiled code's cache epoch

What this does
  - `js_aot_unbind` and the rebinding at the top of `js_aot_bind` move `aot_pic_epoch` (and count it in `aot_epoch_bumps`) when the bytecode
    had a bound entry.

Why we need it
  A compiled method call that identifies its callee by the entry pointer a method table recorded at its entry, instead of testing the
  function object, holds only while that function stays bound. The binding was the one thing about a function value that could change
  without any cache epoch moving.

Functions touched
  `js_aot_unbind`, `js_aot_bind`.

No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -64664,0 +64665,6 @@
+    /* A compiled body that identified a callee by its bound entry (a method table's entry pointer) trusts
+       it until the cache epoch moves, so dropping the binding moves it. */
+    if (b->aot_fn != NULL) {
+        b->realm->rt->aot_epoch_bumps++;
+        b->realm->rt->aot_pic_epoch++;
+    }
@@ -64794,0 +64801,4 @@
+    if (b->aot_fn != NULL) {
+        b->realm->rt->aot_epoch_bumps++;
+        b->realm->rt->aot_pic_epoch++;
+    }
